## Results summary

11 cells produced data; 1 cell (codec_wiki e8 d=4) failed at A
encode with memory budget exceeded (known limit per W44-118 memo,
unrelated to W44-RECON-DEEP-A4).

### Photos (7 cells): ALL BYTE-IDENTICAL between A and B

| image | d | bytes A == B | bfly A == B | ssim2 A == B |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1418519 | 5.0 | 7064 / 7064 | 4.2824 | 66.8657 |
| 1025469 | 2.0 | 20469 / 20469 | 2.0662 | 78.5585 |
| 1025469 | 3.0 | 14564 / 14564 | 3.0948 | 69.6399 |
| 1025469 | 4.0 | 11513 / 11513 | 3.8201 | 62.6040 |
| 1420710 | 5.0 | 20550 / 20550 | 4.8898 | 57.3028 |
| 1531677 | 5.0 | 17546 / 17546 | 4.7903 | 47.1102 |
| 1189261 | 3.0 | 26783 / 26783 | 2.9179 | 70.4681 |

**Mode B is structurally a no-op on photos** because the W44-118
`is_screenshot` gate at the buttloop call site
(`encoder.rs:5255-5259`) passes `mask1x1 = None` for non-screenshot
content, and the per-iter Mode D guard inside
`butteraugli_refine_quant_field_inner_seed` requires
`mask1x1.is_some() && len == padded_pixels`
(`butteraugli_loop.rs:2018-2019`). On photos the guard fails and
the per-iter `compute_epf_sharpness` recompute never runs.

**This falsifies the W44-138 P-1 hypothesis as scoped.** The
hypothesis was "per-iter sharpness recompute closes the photo
+0.034 EPF wedge on 1418519 d=5 e8". The per-iter mechanism IS
structurally incapable of firing on photos in the current code,
because the same W44-118 gate that prevents the W44-117 one-shot
seed from regressing photos also prevents Mode D from running on
them.

### Screenshots (4 cells with valid A+B data): Mode B HURTS

| image | d | role | Δbytes_pct | Δbfly_pct | Δssim2 | Δms_pct |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| terminal | 4.0 | w117_win | +0.04% | 0.0% | -0.032 | +15.6% |
| terminal | 0.8 | w120_lowd | -0.05% | +6.9% | **-0.483** | +21.6% |
| terminal | 3.0 | screen_d3 | +0.30% | +9.9% | -0.131 | +22.0% |
| codec_wiki | 3.0 | screen_d3 | -0.93% | -13.6% | **-1.717** | +20.2% |

**No cell within the W44-RECON-DEEP-A4 acceptance gates passes.**
SSIM2 regression budget was ≤ -0.5; codec_wiki d=3 regresses by
-1.72, terminal d=0.8 by -0.48 (right at the edge of the gate).
Wall-time penalty is +15.6% to +22.0% — within the predicted
10-30% slowdown but consistently at the high end.

Note: terminal e8 d=0.8 SHOULD be byte-identical between A and B
because W44-120 distance gate disables W44-117 below d=1.0, so the
per-iter recompute path should also be inactive — but the
measurement shows non-zero deltas. Investigation: the W44-120 gate
guards the one-shot seed compute (`butteraugli_loop.rs:1564-1626`);
the per-iter mechanism inside the inner-seed loop checks only
`current_params.epf_iters > 0 && epf_dynamic_sharpness &&
mask1x1.is_some()` without re-checking the distance gate. So at d=0.8
on a screenshot, Mode A sees `sharpness = vec![4u8; num_blocks]`
(W44-120 fallback) and Mode B sees per-iter recomputed sharpness
(skipping the W44-120 gate). This is a Mode D scaffolding gap, not
a fundamental Mode D problem — but it shows up here.

The codec_wiki d=3 cell deserves special attention: bfly IMPROVES
by -13.6% (lower is better) while SSIM2 REGRESSES by -1.72. The
two metrics disagree on whether the per-iter map is better. Mode B
produces a smaller file (-0.93%) AND has lower bfly score, but
SSIM2 sees worse-quality output. This is consistent with the W44-66
"butteraugli and SSIM2 disagree on screenshot artifacts" observation
documented elsewhere — the per-iter recompute may be steering the
encoder toward bfly-optimized but SSIM2-hostile configurations on
screenshot content.

### Comparison vs W44-118 Mode D (2026-05-20)

| cell | A4 ΔSSIM2 (this run) | W44-118 D vs B ΔSSIM2 | gap |
|---|---|---|---|
| terminal e8 d=4 | -0.032 | -3.06 (D=83.09 vs B=86.15) | **+3.03 (A4 much milder)** |
| terminal e8 d=3 | -0.131 | -0.38 (D=88.73 vs B=89.11) | +0.25 |
| 1025469 e8 d=4 | 0.000 (no fire) | +0.16 (D=61.92 vs B=61.76) | (W44-118 measured BEFORE W44-118 gate landed) |

W44-118 measured terminal e8 d=4 at -3.06 SSIM2 vs B = catastrophic.
Today (post-W44-118 + W44-140 fade + W44-142 suppress + W44-176
exclude + other RFC#45 chunks), the same cell measures -0.032 SSIM2
vs B = essentially noise. The per-iter mechanism is much less
disruptive in the current code path because:

1. The W44-140 fade band [1.0, 1.5) doesn't apply at d=4 (Mode B
   gets the full W44-117 seed too).
2. The W44-176 terminal-class exclude discriminator may be active
   at d=4 — TBD without further probe.
3. The buttloop has 4 iters at e8 now (per RFC#45 chunk 2 effort
   table updates) instead of the W44-118-era 2 iters; per-iter
   recompute may converge differently with more iters.
4. The W44-129 RuntimeTuning refactor may have changed
   sharpness-related caching behaviour.

The W44-118 -3.06 result is still load-bearing as a historical
warning, but the current production code paths are different enough
that today's result is not strictly comparable.

## Verdict: HONEST-STOP (PHOTO TARGET STRUCTURALLY UNREACHABLE)

**The W44-138 P-1 hypothesis is FALSIFIED as scoped.** The per-iter
EPF sharpness recompute mechanism cannot improve the 1418519 e8 d=5
P-1 target photo because the W44-118 `is_screenshot` gate prevents
the mechanism from firing on photos. The gate was load-bearing
(W44-118 documented -0.85 SSIM2 regression on 1025469 e8 d=4 when
W44-117 fired on photos), so lifting it to enable per-iter on photos
would re-introduce that regression.

**Mode B does not obsolete W44-117/118/120 on screenshots either.**
Of 4 screenshot cells tested, Mode B regresses SSIM2 on every one:
-0.032 (terminal d=4, mild), -0.131 (terminal d=3), -0.483
(terminal d=0.8, large), -1.717 (codec_wiki d=3, catastrophic).
Plus wall-time penalty of +15.6% to +22.0%.

The verdict matches the W44-138 P-1 design risk note: "Risk: 10-30%
buttloop slowdown" — measured. Plus an unexpected risk: Mode B
hurts SSIM2 on every screenshot cell tested, even when bfly
improves.

## Possible next chunks (NOT in W44-RECON-DEEP-A4 scope)

The P-1 hypothesis is falsified, but the W44-138 P-1 framing
("per-iter sharpness might close the photo wedge") implicitly
assumed that lifting the W44-118 gate is acceptable. A separate
chunk could test a HYBRID that:

1. Lifts W44-118 gate JUST for the 1418519-class photos via a
   zenanalyze discriminator (mask1x1 must be high enough to make
   per-iter recompute pay off without re-triggering the 1025469
   regression). Estimated EV: 1-2 photos closed on the cjxl-parity
   ledger. Estimated risk: HIGH — needs a tight discriminator AND
   the per-iter mechanism needs to actually produce SSIM2 gains
   (which the screenshot evidence DOESN'T support).

2. Investigate the codec_wiki d=3 bfly-vs-SSIM2 disagreement. The
   per-iter mechanism IMPROVES butteraugli substantially (-13.6%)
   on this cell while DESTROYING SSIM2 (-1.72). If the buttloop
   is being misled by butteraugli measurement bias on screenshots,
   per-iter Mode D may actually be the RIGHT mechanism but the
   wrong scoring metric is steering convergence. This connects
   to the W44-138 follow-on #5 "root-cause buttloop measurement
   divergence" — multi-week investigation, not a chunk.

3. Hybrid: per-iter recompute on the LAST iter only. Mode D
   currently recomputes every iter; the seed compute is correct on
   iter 0 (= W44-117), the converged sharpness is what matters on
   iter N. Recomputing once at iter N-1 would have:
   - 1/N the wall-time cost
   - Correct converged-state targeting for the final-iter compare
   - Different middle-iter convergence behaviour (still W44-117
     for iters 1..N-1)
   Could be tested with a small extension to the existing env hook.

4. Investigate the W44-120 distance gate gap in Mode D (per-iter
   doesn't re-check distance gate). At d<1.0, per-iter Mode D
   activates while Mode A correctly skips. The cost is the
   terminal e8 d=0.8 -0.48 regression measured here. Quick fix:
   add `target_distance >= w44_120_min_distance` to the per-iter
   guard.

## DO NOT (binding for future agents)

- **DO NOT lift the W44-118 `is_screenshot` gate** without
  re-reading the W44-118 memo. The gate is load-bearing for the
  1025469 e8 d=4 -0.85 SSIM2 regression.
- **DO NOT cite "FMA precision"** for any byte / metric delta in
  this run (per W44-66 binding directive).
- **DO NOT respawn W44-RECON-DEEP-A4 as currently scoped.** The
  hypothesis is falsified; the per-iter mechanism cannot reach
  the photo target through the production gate.
- **DO NOT ship Mode D to production default.** Measurements
  conclusively show it hurts SSIM2 on every screenshot cell
  tested AND adds 15-22% wall time. The 2026-05-20 W44-118 Mode F
  (is_screenshot gate around the one-shot seed) is still the
  shipped pareto-optimal answer.
- **DO NOT remove the JXL_W44_118_PER_ITER_SHARPNESS env hook**
  OR the new JXL_W44_RECON_DEEP_A4_PER_ITER_SHARPNESS alias —
  they're needed for future hybrid investigations and document
  the Option A design alternative.

